WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.639
Today’s guest video is a little different
from normal,

00:00:02.639 --> 00:00:07.029
so be prepared for music, emoji and being
told you’re hot,

00:00:07.029 --> 00:00:10.320
as Alex from Play the Mind takes on song translations.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:11.920
Alex, take it away.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:18.020
♫ Give your heart and soul to me

00:00:18.029 --> 00:00:20.890
♫ and life will always be

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:25.600
♫ La Vie En Rose. ♫

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.640
You just heard a contrafactum.

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:31.400
That’s when the lyrics of the song are changed,
but the music stays the same.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:36.260
Parody songs are a good example of contrafactum
and so are song translations.

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:39.800
But as opposed to parodies, song translations
give the false impression

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:41.980
of remaining faithful to the original.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:42.980
For instance,

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:46.600
what I just sang is part of the English version
of the French song

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:48.500
La Vie En Rose by Edith Piaf.

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:50.240
The French lyrics of what I sang are,

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:56.280
"Et dès que je l'aperçois alors je sens
en moi, mon cœur qui bat."

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:59.190
The meaning of these words has nothing to
do with what I just sang.

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:04.410
As a matter of fact, the whole English version
ends up becoming short, bilingual,

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:07.710
gender-neutral and sung directly to the loved
one,

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:11.969
which are characteristics not present in the
original French version.

00:01:11.969 --> 00:01:14.509
And this song isn’t the only case where
this happens.

00:01:14.509 --> 00:01:16.439
When dealing with any song translation,

00:01:16.439 --> 00:01:20.659
there’s a conflict between the meaning of
the lyrics and the way they sound when spoken.

00:01:20.659 --> 00:01:24.939
If you were to translate a song purely based
on meaning,

00:01:24.939 --> 00:01:28.680
you would end up having lyrics with beats
following on syllables,

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:31.590
which naturally don't RE-ceive an ac-CENT
when spo-KEN.

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:37.070
So, somewhere, you need to sacrifice some
meaning in order to preserve good sound.

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:42.320
And who’s to blame for making song translations
so darn complicated?

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:45.350
It’s phonology.

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:48.569
The phonology of a language is like its musical
DNA

00:01:48.569 --> 00:01:50.159
and it has a primordial influence

00:01:50.159 --> 00:01:53.759
on how the melody of a song gets written in
a certain language.

00:01:53.759 --> 00:01:57.549
Even the particular way a baby cries has to
do with how its mother speaks.

00:01:57.549 --> 00:02:00.549
There are two things to consider in phonology.

00:02:00.549 --> 00:02:03.240
The phonemes are the vowels and consonants
that make up speech

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:06.670
and the prosody of a language is the way the
phonemes are put together

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:10.080
into syllables, words and sentences and their
intonation

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:11.590
in order to convey meaning.

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:14.740
It’s the prosody of English that makes the
difference between saying,

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:19.880
“Hey, you’re HOT,” and saying,
“Hey. YOU’RE hot.”

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:22.840
But, back to music.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:25.110
It’s the phonological system of a language

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:28.870
which shapes the melody of a song originally
written in that language.

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:30.799
And when translating that song into a language

00:02:30.799 --> 00:02:33.939
with a completely different phonological system,

00:02:33.939 --> 00:02:36.730
something ends up sounding bad.

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:39.540
For instance, some languages, like Hindi or
Italian,

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:41.930
are more favourable to melismatic singing,

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:45.319
where you have multiple notes sung over just
a few vowels.

00:02:45.319 --> 00:02:48.660
Here’s an example in English:

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:52.040
♫ If I...

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:55.640
♫ should stay... ♫

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:58.340
That was Whitney Houston.

00:02:58.349 --> 00:03:00.969
And other languages, such as German or Polish,

00:03:00.969 --> 00:03:03.510
are more favourable to syllabic singing,

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:06.000
where you have one note per syllable.

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:09.160
♫ Freude schöner Götterfunken

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:12.120
♫ Tochter aus Elysium! ♫

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:13.980
That was Ludwig Van.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:15.980
Gesundheit!

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:20.660
Therein lies the crux of the matter.

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:23.980
Setting the text of a syllabic language to
a melismatic song

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:27.360
or vice-versa, sounds forced, artificial.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:31.390
I’m fully aware that no language is purely
syllabic or purely melismatic,

00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:35.769
but I am emphasising these extremes in order
to drive my point home.

00:03:35.769 --> 00:03:40.130
A song translation can either sound good,
but be inaccurate in meaning,

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:43.640
or it can be accurate, but sound bad.

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:44.640
Take your pick.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:47.480
But, how important are song translations nowadays?

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:49.239
Well, think of this.

00:03:49.239 --> 00:03:52.871
Roughly 80 million people speak Korean worldwide,
yet to this day,

00:03:52.871 --> 00:03:57.290
the video on YouTube with most views is the
Korean song ‘Gangnam Style’

00:03:57.290 --> 00:03:59.519
with more than 2.5 billion views.

00:03:59.519 --> 00:04:04.890
This means most of the audience probably didn’t
understand a word that was being sung.

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:08.879
Go subscribe to Play the Mind for all sorts
of videos about music theory and acoustics,

00:04:08.879 --> 00:04:11.829
including one about the very first mic drop.

00:04:11.829 --> 00:04:14.280
Next time, in the last guest video of the
run,

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:16.449
we have something in the ever-popular genre
of

00:04:16.449 --> 00:04:19.310
"that thing you’re eating isn’t what you
think it is".

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:19.810
See you then.

